---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Suslify | Why We Built It"
  description="Suslify was built to solve a simple problem: too many people have usable items they don't need, and too many non-profits need exactly those items but can't reach donors."
>
  <Header />

  <section class="relative overflow-hidden bg-white pt-12 pb-16 md:pt-16">
    <div class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="absolute -top-32 -right-32 w-[500px] h-[500px] rounded-full bg-brand-100/50 blur-3xl"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="relative max-w-3xl mx-auto px-5">

      <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-600 mb-4">Our story</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 mb-8 leading-tight">
        What started as a school project<br />
        <span class="text-brand-500">became something real.</span>
      </h1>

      <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none text-slate-600 leading-relaxed space-y-6 text-[1.05rem]">
        <p>
          Two years ago, the founder of Suslify was a high school senior looking for a project at the intersection of two things she cared deeply about: social impact and mobile application development. She wanted to build something that actually mattered, not just something that looked good on a submission.
        </p>

        <p>
          Growing up, she noticed something in the community around her. Communities look out for each other. People help family, friends, neighbors. But it was rarely organized. There was no easy way to connect someone with a box of clothes they no longer needed to an organization that was actively looking for exactly that.
        </p>

        <p>
          The more she looked into it, the more she saw a real gap. There are platforms that help large corporations donate surplus inventory. There are community tools for passing things between neighbors. But for everyday people and local nonprofits, item donation is still fragmented and mostly invisible. Unlike money, you can't click a button and send a coat to a family that needs one. You have to know where to go, who to call, and hope it works out.
        </p>

        <p>
          Many people genuinely want to help but don't know where to start. Many non-profits struggle to communicate what they actually need. And even when both sides are trying, connecting them is harder than it should be. Usable goods get thrown away. Needs go unmet. Not because people don't care, but because the infrastructure to match them doesn't exist.
        </p>

        <p>
          That's the problem Suslify is built to solve. A real-time platform where donors can post what they have, non-profits can request what they need, and both sides can connect, message, and complete a donation without the friction. Free for everyone. Simple by design.
        </p>

        <p>
          What started as a senior project has slowly grown into something much bigger. Still early, but the direction is clear. Item donation should be as easy as any other form of giving.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 p-7 bg-brand-50 rounded-2xl border border-brand-100">
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Got a question or want to partner?</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-4">We'd love to hear from you.</p>
        <a href="mailto:hello@suslify.com"
           class="inline-flex bg-brand-500 hover:bg-brand-600 text-white font-bold text-sm px-5 py-2.5 rounded-full transition-all shadow-md shadow-brand-500/20">
          hello@suslify.com
        </a>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
